      "problem": "Consider the following polynomial division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x-14 \\\\  & x+2\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-2{x}^{2}-18x-\\phantom{1}1\\,\\,\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-2{x}^{2}-4x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-14x-1 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-14x-28}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the above, $\\frac{-2{x}^{2}-18x-1}{x+2}=$",
      "worked_solution": "$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x-14 \\\\  & x+2\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-2{x}^{2}-18x-\\phantom{1}1\\,\\,\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-2{x}^{2}-4x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-14x-1 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-14x-28}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given polynomial division, we deduce that dividing the polynomial $-2{x}^{2}-18x-1$ by the linear binomial $x+2$ gives\n\n- the quotient $-2x-14,$ and\n\n- the remainder $27.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{-2{x}^{2}-18x-1}{x+2}=-2x-14+\\frac{27}{x+2}.\n$$",
